==Finnish==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|fi}} {{term|herra|lang=non}}, from {{etyl|osx|fi}} {{term|herro|hērro|lang=osx}}, from {{etyl|goh|fi}} {{term|heriro|hēriro|lang=odt}}, {{term|herro|hērro|lang=goh}}, the comparative form of {{term|her|hēr|noble, venerable|lang=goh}} (German {{term|hehr|lang=de}}), by analogy with Latin {{term|senior||elder|lang=la}}. Cognates include Danish, Norwegian and Swedish {{term|herre|lang=sv}}, Icelandic {{term|herra|lang=is}}, Dutch {{term|heer|lang=nl}}, German {{term|Herr|lang=de}}. The Old High German word originally meant "grey, grey-haired", and descends from {{etyl|gem-pro|fi}} {{recons|hairaz||grey|lang=gem-pro}}, making it cognate with Old English {{term|har|hār|lang=ang}} (English {{term|hoar|lang=en}}), Old Norse {{term|hárr|lang=non}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|Fi-herra.ogg|Audio}}
* {{hyphenation|her|ra}}
* {{rhymes|lang=fi|erːɑ}}
* {{IPA|lang=fi|/ˈherːɑ/}}

===Noun===
{{fi-noun}}

# [[mister]], [[gentleman]], [[sir]] (polite address)
#: ''Anteeksi, '''herra''' ...''
#:: Excuse me, '''sir''' ...
#: ''Hyvät '''herrat'''!''
#:: '''Gentlemen'''!
#: '''''Herra''' Virtanen, voinko puhua kanssanne?''
#:: '''Mister''' Virtanen, may I speak with you?
# [[lord]] (person having formal authority over others)
# lord (person enjoying great respect in a community)
# lord, [[master]] (owner)
# capitalized ({{term|Herra|lang=fi}}), [[Lord]] (God)

