==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|is}} {{term|herra|lang=non}}, from {{etyl|osx|is}} {{term|herro|hērro|lang=osx}}, from {{etyl|goh|is}} {{term|heriro|hēriro|lang=odt}}, {{term|herro|hērro|lang=goh}}, the comparative form of {{term|her|hēr|noble, venerable|lang=goh}} (German {{term|hehr|lang=de}}), by analogy with Latin {{term|senior||elder|lang=la}}. Cognates include Danish, Norwegian and Swedish {{term|herre|lang=sv}}, Dutch {{term|heer|lang=nl}}, German {{term|Herr|lang=de}}. The Old High German word originally meant "grey, grey-haired", and descends from {{etyl|gem-pro|is}} {{recons|hairaz||grey|lang=gem-pro}}, making it cognate with Old English {{term|har|hār|lang=ang}} (English {{term|hoar|lang=en}}), Old Norse {{term|hárr|lang=non}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhɛrːa/|lang=is}}
*: {{rhymes|ɛrːa|lang=is}}

===Noun===
{{is-noun|m|herra|herrar}}

# [[lord]], [[master]]
# the [[Lord]] (God)
# [[mister]], [[gentleman]]
# [[sir]]

====Declension====
{{is-decl-noun-m-w1a|h|e|rr}}

====Usage notes====
Used as a title (prenominally or on its own) for bishops and for the president of Iceland (also, especially historically, for some other dignitaries). Also used as a general courtesy title for men, equivalent to English {{term|mister|lang=en}}, but then usually only in formal written correspondence and abbreviated {{term|Hr.|lang=is}} However, when it is used as an honorary title for bishops, presidents, etc., it should never be abbreviated. The equivalent female title is {{term|frú|lang=is}} (in both contexts).

====See also====
* {{context|archaic|lang=is}} [[herri]]

====Derived terms====
* [[andlangs herra]]
* [[dömur mínar og herrar]]
* [[herramaður]]
* [[herramannsmatur]]

===Verb===
{{is-verb-weak|herraði|herrað}}

# to address or refer to as [[herra]] (as should be done to bishops, etc.)

====Conjugation====
